From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 12:11:22 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] usb_canbus: Reject unsupported host channels

The bridge reports a single gs_usb CAN channel, numbered zero, but accepted bulk frames carrying any channel value.  It then ignored that selector while transmitting the frame and echoed the invalid value unchanged.

The Linux gs_usb receive path treats an echoed channel outside the advertised range as a broken device and detaches the network interface.  Thus one malformed host frame could be reflected into a device-level failure instead of being contained at the USB boundary.

Discard bulk frames for channels the bridge does not advertise before they enter the routing queue.  A detailed source comment records both the gs_usb invariant and the otherwise non-obvious Linux failure mode.

Signed-off-by: JR Lomas <lomas.jr@gmail.com>

diff --git a/src/generic/usb_canbus.c b/src/generic/usb_canbus.c
index 7816b08f..ef707b6b 100644
--- a/src/generic/usb_canbus.c
+++ b/src/generic/usb_canbus.c
@@ -211,6 +211,18 @@ drain_usb_host_messages(void)
          */
         if (ret != sizeof(*gs) || gs->can_dlc > sizeof(gs->data))
             continue;
+        /*
+         * device_config.icount advertises one CAN channel, so channel zero is
+         * the only value the gs_usb protocol permits for this interface.  The
+         * bridge echoes the host frame before routing it, including this field.
+         * Linux treats an echoed channel outside the advertised range as a
+         * broken device and detaches the CAN network interface.  Discard such
+         * a frame here so malformed USB output cannot be reflected back as an
+         * apparently fatal device response or be transmitted on the physical
+         * bus under a channel selector the firmware silently ignored.
+         */
+        if (gs->channel)
+            continue;
         UsbCan.host_push_pos = push_pos = push_pos + 1;
     }
 }
